o  give you an idea of the type of  advertising they do  they say space technology is the number  one future career for kids your kid can  learn to code a spaceship  in just 46 minutes start free coding and  a space tech trial  okay first of all it's not free it's  about 200 a month and obviously it's  ridiculous that someone can code a  spaceship in 46 minutes  right but they continue with this idea  basically telling you that actually the  fact that your kids so young  doesn't mean that they're hindered from  learning things like ai  and ml it actually means they may learn  it faster and you can see that in their  curriculum where they show you how much  your kids going to be learning and they  tell you things like  uh that your kid will get a game  developer certificate before they've  been through  eight classes of goading so you can have  your six-year-old going through seven  classes and all of a sudden  he's coding up candy crush in league of  legends and this has led many  people to criticize their overly  simplified and almost  dreamlike promises they're making to  parents that their kids going to be  the next bill gates and ads like this  reinforce the point  they're saying that you can make a 1.2  million  dollar salary at age 13. i mean  talk about selling parents a dream  you're saying hey  pay us 200 a month and your kid can  potentially be working at google it's  obviously google's campus  and be making a 1.2 million dollar  salary and this is where a lot of the  criticism is coming from  because not only did they suggest this  as a possibility  they actually said that one of their  students had achieved this  meet wolf gupta an example that white  hat jr was using  apparently he learned ai to get a 1.2  million dollar job and he's 13 at the  time when he did it  now here's where the cracks first start  to appear because later on  in different ads their story started to  change because over here  we have wolf gupta learning computers at  age  nine so 